INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Wirecard supervisory implications policy briefing and Wirecard KPMG announcement", "source_url": "https://safe-frankfurt.de/fileadmin/user_upload/editor_common/Policy_Center/SAFE_Policy_White_Paper_74.pdf; https://www.wirecard.com/2020/04/22/wirecard-ag-kpmg-special-review-lasts-until-april-27-2020-analysis-so-far-provides-no-evidence-of-balance-sheet-manipulation", "text": "SRC-002 (European Parliament ECON-commissioned SAFE policy briefing, manuscript completed May 2020, published November 2020): The authors assess that all five lines of defence failed in one way or another: Wirecard internal control system, supervisory board, external audit, financial-reporting/audit oversight bodies, and BaFin. They say signals suggesting fraudulent behaviour existed years before revelation and identify flawed market/institutional oversight architecture as a root cause, while noting hindsight-bias concerns and presenting an analytical assessment rather than an official finding. History summarized: allegations of accounting inconsistencies in FT from 2015, partly based on Singapore whistleblower information; 2016 Zatarra report accused Wirecard of money laundering and fraud; BaFin referred some short-selling activity to prosecutors for market abuse; after a January 2019 FT report BaFin requested FREP investigation, later restricted net short-sale increases and filed complaints against short sellers and the journalist; FT continued reporting allegedly inflated sales/profits backed by internal documents; Wirecard hired KPMG in October 2019; KPMG report delayed until April 2020 and stated it could not verify existence of €1 billion in foreign bank accounts; BaFin filed a criminal complaint against Wirecard in June 2020; days later Wirecard filed bankruptcy acknowledging €1.9 billion in missing financial assets. SRC-001 (Wirecard/DGAP announcement dated 28-Apr-2020): Wirecard said KPMG's special audit found no incriminating evidence for public allegations of balance-sheet forgery and no significant findings requiring adjustment of 2016-2018 annual accounts across TPA, MCA/Digital Lending, India and Singapore. It said available documents/audit measures had been sufficient to prove TPA revenues in prior annual audits, but KPMG's increased forensic requirements meant not all needed data could be obtained because data were primarily controlled by third parties; Wirecard said it supplied over 200 million December 2019 data records and KPMG found no deviations between reported revenues and account balances. KPMG identified documentation and organisational weaknesses; Wirecard said it was remedying them since 2019. Wirecard delayed 2020 annual accounts/press conference and said it would coordinate with EY on completion."}
